{"authored" [{"question" "q-125585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2+4y^2=36$, oriented counterclockwise. Interpret circulation here as the sum of the four convergent improper line integrals on the open quadrant arcs, taking the one-sided limits independently at each axis intersection. The field is undefined at $(\\pm2,0)$ and $(0,\\pm3)$; ordinary circulation on the entire closed ellipse is not defined as stated in the original source." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/review.json" "file_sha256" "c6b59c695f23af1861c42b5881d87fbacdec14013e9887328b62bd2339983cd5" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "ac68a63e4ade419ee0e6dba7c5a7616a47611ef2404b7abbacf66ce1776ae081" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/verification.json" "basis" 1937 "rationale" "Add the omitted convergent improper-integral interpretation, without changing the field, ellipse, orientation, choices or -10pi answer. Independently convergent open-arc integrals resolve the ambiguity; original ordinary circulation remains undefined."} {"question" "q-125585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The field is undefined where $x=0$ or $y=0$, so the original prompt does not define an ordinary line integral over all of $C$. Under the explicit improper-integral interpretation, parameterize counterclockwise by\n\n$$r(t)=(2\\cos t,3\\sin t),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le2\\pi.$$\n\nFor $t$ strictly inside each of the four quadrant intervals, both coordinates are nonzero and\n\n$$\\frac{3}{y}\\,dx=\\frac{3}{3\\sin t}(-2\\sin t)\\,dt=-2\\,dt,\\qquad -\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy=-\\frac{2}{2\\cos t}(3\\cos t)\\,dt=-3\\,dt.$$\n\nThus the pulled-back integrand is exactly $-5$ on each open arc. At every excluded endpoint its one-sided limit is $-5$, so every individual improper integral converges; no cancellation between divergent integrals or principal-value convention is needed. With $a_k=k\\pi/2$,\n\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{imp}}=\\sum_{k=0}^{3}\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0,\\,\\delta\\downarrow0}\\int_{a_k+\\epsilon}^{a_{k+1}-\\delta}(-5)\\,dt=4\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi}{2}\\right)=-10\\pi.$$\n\nThe unchanged displayed option c is therefore correct for this explicit interpretation. This does not assign values to $F$ at the four axis intersections or establish ordinary circulation there." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/review.json" "file_sha256" "c6b59c695f23af1861c42b5881d87fbacdec14013e9887328b62bd2339983cd5" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "ac68a63e4ade419ee0e6dba7c5a7616a47611ef2404b7abbacf66ce1776ae081" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/verification.json" "basis" 1937 "rationale" "Add the omitted convergent improper-integral interpretation, without changing the field, ellipse, orientation, choices or -10pi answer. Independently convergent open-arc integrals resolve the ambiguity; original ordinary circulation remains undefined."} {"question" "q-125585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10\\pi "] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/review.json" "file_sha256" "c6b59c695f23af1861c42b5881d87fbacdec14013e9887328b62bd2339983cd5" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "ac68a63e4ade419ee0e6dba7c5a7616a47611ef2404b7abbacf66ce1776ae081" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-125585/verification.json" "basis" 1937 "rationale" "Add the omitted convergent improper-integral interpretation, without changing the field, ellipse, orientation, choices or -10pi answer. Independently convergent open-arc integrals resolve the ambiguity; original ordinary circulation remains undefined."} {"question" "q-125585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b9b5f54f71142e3b519c67634b4b8eb18028b59d5a77e955f28f5b94598f30c" "basis" 1936 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-125585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b9b5f54f71142e3b519c67634b4b8eb18028b59d5a77e955f28f5b94598f30c" "basis" 1936 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-125585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0739c96ad346238c47110cc19d8571d34c4c37477e99709f7d89249005e6827" "basis" 1936 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/140602